.TH "CAPNG_GET_CAPS_PROCESS" "3" "June 2009" "Red Hat" "Libcap-ng API"
.SH NAME
capng_get_caps_process \- get the capabilities from a process
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B #include <cap-ng.h>
.sp
int capng_get_caps_process(void);

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

capng_get_caps_process will get the capabilities and bounding set of the pid stored inside libcap-ng's state table. The default is the pid of the running process. This can be changed by using the capng_setpid function.

.SH "RETURN VALUE"

This returns 0 on success and -1 on failure. A failed read invalidates the capability snapshot, so queries cannot return stale capabilities from an earlier successful read.

After a failed capget, callers can retry the read or use capng_setpid to select another target. Queries made without a valid snapshot may attempt a fresh read.

.SH NOTES

If you are doing multi-threaded programming, calling this function will only get capabilities on the calling thread. If you want to get overall capabilities for a multi-threaded process, you can only do that before creating any threads. Afterwards, threads may be able to independently set capabilities.

Reading another thread's bounding or ambient set requires its status file in /proc. If that metadata is unavailable, the call fails rather than substituting the calling thread's sets. For the calling thread, the library can query these sets without /proc.

.SH "SEE ALSO"

.BR capng_setpid (3),
.BR capabilities (7)

